  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe stalled front that served to focus rounds of showers across\nNew Hampshire earlier today remains in place. While the \nsteadiest precip has moved out at this point, scattered showers \nand occasional drizzle will linger and increase in coverage \nsouth of the mountains overnight tonight as the airmass \ncontinues to moisten. Some patchy fog will also be possible \ntonight as well.\n\nThe high pressure across Maine strengthens through tomorrow, \nkeeping northeastern areas brighter and drier for another day. \nFurther west, clouds increase through the daytime as the next \nwave of low pressure approaches from the west. Some scattered \nshowers and sprinkles remain possible through the day tomorrow, \nbut the bulk of the next round of precip holds off until \ntomorrow night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper-level ridge axis will continue to move east of the forecast \narea late Wednesday as a closed low moves into central Quebec. A \nsurface low will also deepen over west central Quebec with a cold \nfront extending to the south southwest and a warm front extending to \nthe southeast. Widespread precipitation chances will increase from \nwest to east Wednesday night in warm air advection as mid-level \nheights fall. A secondary low may then form along the cold front \nover southern Quebec and propagate southeast across northern New \nHampshire and central Maine on Thursday, eventually moving out to \nsea by Friday morning. Another batch of widespread showers and a few \nthunderstorms will accompany this low/frontal passage. While \nmost locations should at least see some measurable rain, \nactivity on Thursday may be a bit more convective and hit or \nmiss at times. Still, the NBM is advertising widespread medium \nto high probabilities (50 to 80 percent) for 24-hour QPF of an \ninch or greater by the time the low moves out to sea Thursday \nnight. Thus, a good soaking rain appears likely for most \nlocations across New Hampshire and western Maine with some \nlocally higher amounts due to the convective nature on Thursday\nand seasonably high PWATS in the forecast. As we move into the \nrange of some long range CAMs, it is apparent that we may see a \nlull or two in activity Wednesday night through Thursday.\n\nA few stronger thunderstorms also can't be ruled out on Thursday \nwith some NAM forecast soundings over southern New Hampshire \nadvertising some modest instability and decent shear. The degree of \ndestabilization remains highly uncertain considering widespread cloud \ncover and rain/showers through the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe upper low moves across Nova Scotia late Friday into \nSaturday, leaving some unsettled conditions in place over the \nmountains with some isolated to scattered diurnal showers \npossible Friday and Saturday afternoon. Persistent northwest \nflow will lead to cooler temperatures and dewpoints through the \nholiday weekend. Saturday and Sunday will feel a little bit like\nearly autumn with highs mainly forecast in the mid 60s north to\nthe mid 70s south and some breezier winds out of the north. An \napproaching ridge may lead to a slight warmup on Labor Day with \nsome low precipitation chances returning to the forecast Monday \nnight into Tuesday.",
